2	 * Bulk-import real GitHub-hosted game mods/plugins into `GameMod` — the
3	 * GitHub-native counterpart to `scripts/import-modrinth-catalog.ts`, for
4	 * ecosystems Modrinth/CurseForge/Thunderstore don't cover (CS2's native
5	 * Metamod/CounterStrikeSharp/SwiftlyS2 plugin scene is the first and, this
6	 * pass, the only one actually run — see "WHAT ACTUALLY RAN" below).
7	 *
8	 * WHY THIS EXISTS. `prisma/seed-gamemods.ts` hand-seeded 22 CS2 rows with
9	 * `source: "github"`, and `scripts/verify-gamemods.ts`'s own header comment
10	 * says GitHub was deliberately left unwired ("no lightweight give-me-this-
11	 * project's-metadata endpoint the way Modrinth/Spiget do... wiring those up is
12	 * real, separate follow-up work") — which is why all 22 rows have sat with
13	 * `license: null` and `verifiedAt: null` since they were seeded. That's not
14	 * just a cosmetic gap: CS2's ecosystem is unlicensed-by-default far more often
15	 * than Modrinth's (plenty of real, popular, actively-maintained CS2 plugin
16	 * repos ship with no LICENSE file at all), and this catalogue makes no
17	 * copyright claim either way without checking. This script is that follow-up:
18	 * it verifies/refreshes every existing GitHub-sourced row for a family AND
19	 * discovers new ones, both through the same real GitHub REST API.
20	 *
21	 * SOURCE: GitHub REST API v3, https://docs.github.com/en/rest — plus, for CS2
22	 * specifically, a real curated seed list (github.com/samyycX/awesome-cs2's
23	 * README, fetched raw off raw.githubusercontent.com — not the GitHub API, so
24	 * it costs nothing against the API rate limit) which is more reliable for
25	 * this game than topic search alone (verified live 2026-09-20: search only
26	 * turns up 57 `cs2-plugin`-tagged repos; the curated list has 121 across its
27	 * three real plugin-framework sections and catches things authors never
28	 * tagged).
29	 *
30	 * TWO KINDS OF WORK PER GAME, run together in one pass:
31	 *   1. VERIFY existing rows — every GameMod row already in this family with
32	 *      source "github" gets looked up by its real `sourceId` ("owner/repo")
33	 *      and, if the repo still resolves, has its verified-fact fields
34	 *      (license, description, iconUrl/authorAvatarUrl, pushed-date-derived
35	 *      upstreamOk, ...) refreshed. Hand-curated fields — name, category,
36	 *      popularity, featured — are NEVER touched here; only
37	 *      scripts/verify-gamemods.ts's "verified upstream facts" section is.
38	 *      A repo that 404s gets `upstreamOk: false` (never deleted — same
39	 *      convention as verify-gamemods.ts).
40	 *   2. DISCOVER new rows — real repos found via a curated list and/or GitHub
41	 *      topic search that pass the quality gate and aren't already in the DB
42	 *      (by sourceId) get created fresh, with name/category/popularity/loaders
43	 *      derived from real signals (repo name, curated section, topics,
44	 *      stargazers_count — never fabricated).
45	 *
46	 * MATCHING KEY: `sourceId` ("owner/repo", GitHub's own `full_name`), looked up
47	 * case-insensitively — NOT the `slug` the `@@unique([gameFamily, slug])`
48	 * constraint is actually built on. GitHub repos get renamed/transferred more
49	 * than Modrinth slugs do, and matching on a stable API-native identifier
50	 * before falling back to slug generation avoids ever creating a second row
51	 * for the same repo just because this script's slugify differs from
52	 * whichever human picked the original curated slug (e.g. seed-gamemods.ts's
53	 * "vipcore" for `partiusfabaa/cs2-vip` — a curated display name, not a
54	 * mechanical slugify of the repo name).
55	 *
56	 * QUALITY GATE (spam filter). Per this session's own research: star count
57	 * ALONE is not reliable (a suspicious high-star/no-description repo turned up
58	 * in a live topic search this session). A repo is imported only if ALL of:
59	 *   - it has a real description (>= 15 chars, not just whitespace/emoji)
60	 *   - it is not archived
61	 *   - it was pushed to within the last MAX_STALE_DAYS (730 days / ~2 years)
62	 * Star count is stored as a real signal (capped `popularity`), never used as
63	 * the sole admission test.
64	 *
65	 * LICENSE GATE — Nxeon legal policy set this session, applied literally:
66	 * GitHub content is MIRROR-SAFE only when the repo's own license object
67	 * carries a confirmed permissive SPDX id (see PERMISSIVE_SPDX below).
68	 * `spdx_id: "NOASSERTION"` (GitHub's "other"/could-not-classify bucket) and no
69	 * license at all are both treated as NOT confirmed-permissive. Per this
70	 * session's explicit instruction, non-permissive repos are not skipped: they
71	 * are imported "link-out-only" (same fetch-not-mirror pattern this codebase
72	 * already uses for non-permissive Thunderstore/Modrinth content — we only
73	 * ever store metadata + a live-resolved link to the AUTHOR'S OWN GitHub
74	 * release asset, worker/game-mods.ts's resolveUrl(), never a rehosted copy),
75	 * with the real license string stored as-is and `upstreamNote` saying plainly
76	 * that it isn't confirmed permissive. The `license` field NEVER says
77	 * something is permissive when it isn't — see `classifyLicense` below.
78	 *
79	 * WHAT THIS DOES NOT FETCH (by design, see resolveUrl() in
80	 * worker/game-mods.ts): the latest release / its download asset. Resolving a
81	 * download is the WORKER's job, done fresh at install time (GET
82	 * /repos/{owner}/{repo}/releases/latest) — exactly like modrinth's resolveUrl
83	 * already does — so nothing here goes stale the moment an author cuts a new
84	 * release, and this script's own GitHub API budget (see next section) isn't
85	 * spent on a call whose result would just be thrown away by the time anyone
86	 * actually installs the mod.
87	 *
88	 * RATE LIMIT — THE BINDING CONSTRAINT THIS PASS. Confirmed live 2026-09-20:
89	 * no GITHUB_TOKEN in /opt/nxeon/.env, so this runs unauthenticated: 60 REST
90	 * requests/hour (core), 10 search requests/minute — a real, tight budget
91	 * against ~150+ real CS2 plugin repos across the curated list + topic search.
92	 * Two things stretch it a long way:
93	 *   - GitHub's `/search/repositories` response embeds the FULL repo object
94	 *     (license, description, pushed_at, stargazers_count, owner.avatar_url,
95	 *     topics, archived) per hit — confirmed live this session. Any repo that
96	 *     turns up in a topic search costs ZERO extra core requests; only repos
97	 *     found solely via the curated list (not also topic-tagged) need a real
98	 *     `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}` call.
99	 *   - `GhClient` tracks the real `X-RateLimit-Remaining` header and stops
100	 *     ACCEPTING NEW WORK once remaining drops to `coreSafetyMargin` (default
101	 *     5) rather than sleeping up to an hour for the window to reset — a
102	 *     bulk-import script blocking this long is worse than finishing a smaller
103	 *     real slice and reporting exactly what it deferred, which is what
104	 *     `--apply` runs of this script do (see FINAL SUMMARY's "deferred"
105	 *     lines). Re-run later (ideally with a token — see GITHUB_TOKEN below)
106	 *     to pick up where it left off; it is idempotent, so partial coverage is
107	 *     never partial correctness.
108	 *   - Set `GITHUB_TOKEN` in the environment (a plain read-only PAT is enough
109	 *     — this script only ever does GET requests) to raise the core budget to
110	 *     5000/hour; nothing else about this script changes.
111	 *
112	 * MULTI-GAME SHAPE, SINGLE GAME RUN. `GAME_CONFIGS` below has real entries for
113	 * cs2, minecraft, gmod, tmodloader (terraria), and valheim — the five this
114	 * session's research prioritized — each with its own real topic tag(s) and
115	 * (cs2 only) a real curated seed list. WHAT ACTUALLY RAN THIS SESSION,
116	 * 2026-09-20, was `--game=cs2` only: the unauthenticated budget above is
117	 * consumed by CS2 alone (150+ real candidate repos), so minecraft/gmod/
118	 * tmodloader/valheim were never even dry-run — attempting one costs real core
